\def\magnification{\afterassignment\m@g\count@}
\def\m@g{\mag\count@
  \hsize6.5truein\vsize8.9truein\dimen\footins8truein}

\def\loggingall{\tracingcommands\tw@\tracingstats\tw@
  \tracingpages\@ne\tracingoutput\@ne\tracinglostchars\@ne
  \tracingmacros\tw@\tracingparagraphs\@ne\tracingrestores\@ne
  \showboxbreadth\maxdimen\showboxdepth\maxdimen}
\def\tracingall{\tracingonline\@ne\loggingall}

\def\showhyphens#1{\setbox0\vbox{\parfillskip\z@skip\hsize\maxdimen\tenrm
  \pretolerance\m@ne\tolerance\m@ne\hbadness0\showboxdepth0\ #1}}

\normalbaselines\rm % select roman font
\nonfrenchspacing % punctuation affects the spacing
\catcode`@=12 % at signs are no longer letters

\def\fmtname{plain}\def\fmtversion{3.1415926535} % identifies the current format

% ---- the text layer, with the default output routine
\def\fz{lazy}\newif\iffz \fztrue
\message{[part 0 story]}
\vsize=5in
\headline={\tenrm\firstmark\hfil\botmark}
\hrule
\vskip 1in
\centerline{\bf A SHORT STORY}
\vskip 6pt
\centerline{\sl by A. U. Thor}
\vskip .5cm
\mark{Galaxy}Once upon a time, in a distant
  galaxy called \"O\"o\c c,
there lived a computer
named R.~J. Drofnats.\footnote*{A computer of the old school, with
a front panel of blinking lights.}

Mr.~Drofnats---or ``R. J.,'' as
he preferred to be called---was happiest when he was at work
typesetting beautiful documents.\footnote\dag{Mostly cookbooks.}

\medskip
\noindent{\bf EXERCISE 1.1}\quad\mark{Exercise}Explain why R.~J. was happiest
at work, and what ``beautiful'' means for a document set by a computer. Give
two reasons, and a third if you can find one.

\smallskip
\item{a)} Typesetting is a craft, and craftsmen like their work.
\item{b)} The documents were beautiful, which pleased him.
\vskip 1in
\hrule
\vskip .5in


\message{[part 0 pages]}
\vsize=3in
\def\para#1{#1 This paragraph is one of many that fill several pages of
text set with plain \TeX's macros and its output routine, which adds a
footline with the page number to every page. The page builder breaks the
pages, and the lines are broken by the paragraph builder as usual: with
glue that stretches and shrinks, and penalties for clubs and widows.\par}
\para{One.}\para{Two.}\para{Three.}
\medskip
\para{Four.}\para{Five.}
\bigskip
\para{Six.}\para{Seven.}\para{Eight.}
\smallbreak
\para{Nine.}\para{Ten.}\para{Eleven.}\para{Twelve.}

\para{Thirteen.}\para{Fourteen.}


\message{[part 0 display]}
\vsize=2.5in
Some text before a display of a formula, which is set in display style with
the equation number at the right:
$$\int_0^\infty e^{-x^2}\,dx={\sqrt\pi\over2}.\eqno(1)$$
Then the text goes on, and another display follows with a sum and a
fraction, its number at the left:
$$\leqno(2)\sum_{k=1}^n k={n(n+1)\over2}$$
and a third without a number, with delimiters and a radical:
$$\left[{a\atop c}\right]\qquad\sqrt{x^2+y^2}\le|x|+|y|.$$
More text, enough to go on to a second page with more displays. More text,
enough to go on to a second page with more displays. More text, enough to
go on to a second page with more displays.
$$f(x)=\overline{x^2}+\underline{\hat x}\cdot\tilde y\,\bigl(z_1^2\bigr)$$
$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\left(1+{1\over n}\right)^n=e$$
The end of the text with displays, after which the document ends.
$$\prod_{p\ \rm prime}{1\over1-p^{-s}}=\sum_{n\ge1}n^{-s}\eqno(3)$$


\message{[part 0 table]}
\vsize=4in
\centerline{\bf A table}
\medskip
\halign{\hfil#\quad&#\hfil\quad&\hfil#\cr
\noalign{\hrule\smallskip}
Item&Description&Price\cr
\noalign{\smallskip\hrule\smallskip}
1&A pen&\$1.00\cr
2&A notebook with many pages&\$3.50\cr
3&An eraser&\$0.25\cr
\noalign{\smallskip\hrule}
}
\medskip
\settabs 3\columns
\+First&Second&Third\cr
\+One&Two&Three\cr
\medskip
Alignments in math: a matrix and cases,
$$\pmatrix{a&b\cr c&d\cr}\qquad f(x)=\cases{0,&if $x<0$;\cr x^2,&otherwise.\cr}$$
and aligned equations:
$$\eqalign{a&=b+c\cr d+e&=f.\cr}\eqno(1)$$


\message{[part 0 hyphens]}
\vsize=2in \hsize=3in \tolerance=1000
